From d576c0451c61bde56d3a0c18cfa566901ca96fc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 17:29:50 +0000
Subject: libcamera: Add a LensAperture control and the soft IPA's Aperture
 algorithm

Cameras with a controllable aperture come in two kinds and libcamera
could express neither: there was no aperture control at all, and
CameraLens rejected any lens entity that did not implement
V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE.

Add controls::LensAperture, an f-number. One control covers both kinds
of hardware because ControlInfo can already describe either: a
continuously variable iris reports a min/max range, while a mechanism
with a fixed set of detents reports them in values(). An application can
tell which it is by looking at values(), and a camera with a fixed
aperture advertises nothing at all.

Teach CameraLens about V4L2_CID_IRIS_ABSOLUTE, and require a lens to
implement focus or iris rather than focus specifically. A phone module
with a variable aperture typically wires the blade mechanism as its own
device, separate from the focus actuator, so demanding focus would
reject it.

Since a sensor can now have two useful lens entities, sort them by what
they can do instead of assuming the first one found is the focus lens.
One entity may be both, so the two pointers may alias, and ownership
moves to a vector.

Carry the iris lens's controls to the IPA and its chosen iris position
back, alongside the existing focus path. As with focus, the ControlInfoMap
is handed over at init() as well as configure(), so that an algorithm can
decide while registering controls whether an aperture exists at all.

The iris is passed as its own ControlInfoMap rather than merged into
lensControls. A ControlInfoMap only borrows its ControlIdMap, every V4L2
device owns its own, and the IPA keeps a copy of the map beyond the call,
so a merged map built from two sub-devices would leave a dangling idmap.
Keeping them separate also keeps the returned ControlLists valid, since a
ControlList is likewise tied to one idmap.

While here, only set V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE when the lens actually has
it. The old test was that the map was non-empty, which would set focus on
an iris-only lens.

Map controls::LensAperture onto the iris actuator, supporting both kinds
of hardware from one tuning description.

A camera whose iris sits at a few detents lists them as f-number and
actuator code pairs, and the algorithm advertises exactly that set
through ControlInfo::values() so an application draws a set of choices.
A request that falls between two of them selects the nearest, and the
metadata reports what was actually selected rather than what was asked
for.

A camera with a continuously variable iris instead gives a piecewise
linear map from f-number to actuator code, in the same flat form the Af
algorithm already uses, and gets a plain min/max range.

A camera with no iris registers no control, so applications hide their
aperture UI rather than offering one that moves nothing.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
